{
 "title": "Why Churches Stopped Ringing Bells in Storms",
 "facts": "For centuries Europeans rang church bells during thunderstorms, believing it drove lightning away; many bells were inscribed 'fulgura frango' (I break the lightning). Bell towers were usually the tallest buildings, so they were struck often. A 1784 study by Johann Fischer counted 386 church towers struck by lightning and 103 bell ringers killed in Germany over 33 years. In the 1750s Benjamin Franklin proposed pointed iron rods on roofs connected by a wire to the ground. Some clergy objected; after the 1755 Cape Ann earthquake, Boston minister Thomas Prince suggested Franklin's iron points might have caused it. Many churches were slow to install rods. In 1769 lightning struck the church of San Nazaro in Brescia, Italy, igniting gunpowder stored in its vaults; the explosion destroyed a large part of the city and killed many people (often cited as about 3,000). Lightning follows the easiest conductive path to ground; the rod and wire give it one that bypasses the building.",
